// microcode package embedded in the guest image, and Xenos microcode cannot run
// on D3D9. Each package was dumped byte-exact out of ARTIST_copy.i64
// (scratch/postfx_step5_wave/PACKAGES_DUMP.md -- every dumped size matches the
// byte count the committed call site already records), disassembled with
// tools/assets/shaders/xenos.py, and re-expressed as HLSL in
// tools/assets/shaders/brn_postfx_helper.fx.
//
// THE CONSTANT SURFACE IS THE CONSOLE'S OWN CTAB, NOT A GUESS -- and the PC
// program's variable table was compared against it entry by entry (name,
// register set, register index, register count, class, type): IDENTICAL for all
// five programs, with no float constant on either side that the tree does not
// bind by name and no bind without a partner.
//
// A FINDING THIS DECODE SETTLES. The three helper pixel programs are 9-, 16- and
// 4-tap, counted both from their tfetch instructions (9 / 16 / 4) and from their
// CTAB register counts (4+4+1 / 4+4+4+4 / 4). So the DWARF member names
// m_9tapPixelProgram / m_16tapPixelProgram / m_4tapPixelProgram are CORRECT as
// committed and the "the member names and the shader-variable names disagree"
// note in rwgpfxhelper.cpp was a false alarm: sampleOffsets4x4_1/_2 +
// sampleOffsets4_1 IS the 9-register set Blur9 pushes 4+4+1 rows into.
//
// ALPHA IS NOT RECOVERED -- IT IS INDETERMINATE ON THE CONSOLE. All four console
// pixel programs finish with the scalar unit's `retain_prev export0.___w [= PS]`
// and none of them issues a scalar op that defines PS for the export, so the
// exported alpha is the previous-scalar residual (the same finding the bloom
// decode recorded). The PC programs write 0 so the result is deterministic.
//
